<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>MajiFlow — {{deviceName}}</title>
<style>{{{css}}}</style>
</head>
<body>

<!-- Header with branding -->
<div class="doc-header">
  <div class="logo">{{{logoSvg}}}</div>
  <div>
    <h1>MajiFlow</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">{{deviceName}} — Water System Documentation</div>
    <div class="pills">
        {{#each componentPills}}<span class="pill">{{this.label}}</span>
        {{/each}}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>System Topology</h2>
<div class="diagram">
{{{topologySvg}}}
</div>
<div class="legend">
      {{#each legendItems}}<span class="legend-item">{{{this.svg}}} <span>{{this.label}}</span></span>
      {{/each}}
</div>

{{{boardPinoutSection}}}

<h2>Routes</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Route</th><th>Type</th><th>Source Min</th><th>Dest Max</th><th>Max Runtime</th><th>Runtime Level</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
{{#each routes}}
    <tr>
      <td>{{this.name}}</td>
      <td><span class="badge {{#if this.needsPump}}badge-pump{{else}}badge-gravity{{/if}}">{{#if this.needsPump}}Pumped{{else}}Gravity{{/if}}</span></td>
      <td>{{this.sourceMin}}</td>
      <td>{{this.destMax}}</td>
      <td>{{this.runtime}}</td>
      <td>{{#if this.runtimeLevelOk}}✓{{else}}—{{/if}}</td>
    </tr>
{{/each}}
  </tbody>
</table>

{{#if hasAutomations}}
<h2>Automations</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Name</th><th>Trigger</th><th>Schedule</th><th>Route</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
{{#each automations}}
    <tr><td>{{this.name}}</td><td>{{this.trigger}}</td><td>{{this.days}}</td><td>{{this.routeName}}</td></tr>
{{/each}}
  </tbody>
</table>
{{/if}}

<h2>Home Assistant Entities</h2>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:var(--text-muted);margin-bottom:8px;">These entities are created on the ESP32 and appear in HA automatically. Calibration and override values are adjustable from the HA UI.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Entity</th><th>Type</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
{{#each routeEntities}}
    <tr><td><code>button.…_start_{{this.index}}</code></td><td>Button</td><td>Start route: {{this.name}}</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>button.…_stop_{{this.index}}</code></td><td>Button</td><td>Stop route: {{this.name}}</td></tr>
{{/each}}
{{#each tankCalEntities}}
    <tr><td><code>number.…_{{this.id}}_cal_empty</code></td><td>Number</td><td>{{this.name}} empty voltage calibration</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>number.…_{{this.id}}_cal_full</code></td><td>Number</td><td>{{this.name}} full voltage calibration</td></tr>
{{/each}}
    <tr><td><code>switch.…_safety_override</code></td><td>Switch</td><td>Bypass pre-start level checks (use with caution)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Timing Parameters</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Valve travel time</td><td><strong>{{timing.valve_travel_time}}</strong></td><td>Time allowed for valves to fully open/close</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Flow watchdog</td><td><strong>{{timing.flow_watchdog_seconds}}s</strong></td><td>No-flow duration before fault/tank-full detection</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Flow confirm</td><td><strong>{{timing.flow_confirm_seconds}}s</strong></td><td>Time to confirm flow is established after start</td></tr>
    <tr><td>API watchdog</td><td><strong>{{timing.api_watchdog_seconds}}s</strong></td><td>HA disconnect duration before route is faulted</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sensor update</td><td><strong>{{timing.update_interval}}</strong></td><td>ADC/sensor polling interval</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Firmware Safety</h2>
<div class="safety-grid">
  <div class="safety-card">
    <strong>Pre-start checks</strong>
    <span>Source/dest level thresholds enforced before every start (pump off, sensors reliable)</span>
  </div>
  <div class="safety-card">
    <strong>Flow watchdog</strong>
    <span>No-flow fault after {{timing.flow_watchdog_seconds}}s · Flow confirmed within {{timing.flow_confirm_seconds}}s</span>
  </div>
  <div class="safety-card">
    <strong>Runtime level</strong>
    <span>Active on routes with pump-rated sensors · Clean stop on threshold breach</span>
  </div>
  <div class="safety-card">
    <strong>API watchdog</strong>
    <span>Route faulted if HA disconnects for {{timing.api_watchdog_seconds}}s</span>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>State Machine</h3>
<p>The ESP32 firmware operates a state machine for each route slot. All safety logic runs on-device — no server dependency.</p>
<div class="state-diagram"><span class="hl">IDLE</span> ──→ <span class="hl">PREPARING</span> ──→ <span class="hl">RUNNING</span> ──→ <span class="hl">STOPPING</span> ──→ <span class="hl">IDLE</span>
          │              │
          └──→ <span class="fault">FAULT</span> ←───┘ ──→ <span class="hl">IDLE</span> (fault_reset)</div>

<h4>Tank Sensor Suppression by State</h4>
<p>Tank level readings are suppressed during pump operation because the pump creates pressure artifacts that corrupt readings. Levels are only trusted when the pump is off.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Tank Readings</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-idle">IDLE (0)</span></td><td>Valid</td><td>Pump off, pressure settled</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-info">PREPARING (1)</span></td><td><strong>Suppressed</strong></td><td>Valves moving, minor transients</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-info">RUNNING (2)</span></td><td><strong>Suppressed</strong></td><td>Pump creates pressure artifacts</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-info">STOPPING (3)</span></td><td><strong>Suppressed</strong></td><td>Depressurizing, valves closing</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-fault">FAULT (4)</span></td><td>Valid</td><td>Pump off, valves closed</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Pre-flight Checks</h3>
<p>Before the pump starts, the ESP32 validates conditions while sensors are reliable (pump off, no pressure artifacts).</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Threshold</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Source tank too low</td><td>&lt; 5%</td><td>Reject start (not a fault)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dest tank already full</td><td>&gt; 95%</td><td>Reject start (not a fault)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>System not IDLE</td><td>state ≠ 0</td><td>Reject start</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Invalid route ID</td><td>Out of range</td><td>Reject start</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Runtime Safety Checks</h3>
<p>All checks run on the ESP32 every 2 seconds while in the RUNNING state.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Check</th><th>Fault Code</th><th>Threshold</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Flow watchdog</td><td><span class="badge badge-fault">no_flow (1)</span></td><td>No pulses for {{timing.flow_watchdog_seconds}}s</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Per-route max runtime</td><td><span class="badge badge-fault">max_runtime (2)</span></td><td>Route-specific <code>max_runtime_s</code> ceiling</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>API watchdog</td><td><span class="badge badge-fault">api_lost (3)</span></td><td>HA disconnected for {{timing.api_watchdog_seconds}}s</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Stop Reasons</h3>
<p>Persists across runs for display in Home Assistant.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Reason</th><th>Trigger</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>0</td><td>None</td><td>Initial state</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Manual stop</td><td>User called <code>pump_stop</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Tank full</td><td>Flow confirmed then stopped (backpressure)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>No flow</td><td>Fault: no pulses after confirmation window</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>Max runtime</td><td>Fault: route exceeded time limit</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>API lost</td><td>Fault: HA connection lost</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Fault vs Clean Stop</h3>
<div class="safety-grid">
  <div class="safety-card" style="border-left-color:#dc2626;">
    <strong>Fault Response</strong>
    <span>Kill pump → HA event (<code>esphome.pump_fault</code>) → Close all valves → <strong>FAULT</strong> (latched)<br>Requires <code>fault_reset</code> service call from HA to return to IDLE.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="safety-card" style="border-left-color:#16a34a;">
    <strong>Clean Stop Response</strong>
    <span>HA event (<code>esphome.pump_stopped</code>) → Kill pump → Depressurize 2s → Close all valves → <strong>IDLE</strong></span>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Tank-to-Tank Overflow Protection</h3>
<p>For routes with a destination tank, flow-based "tank full" detection relies on backpressure stopping flow. Open tanks don't create backpressure, so layered mitigations apply:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Pre-start check</strong>: dest tank &gt; 95% → reject start</li>
  <li><strong>Per-route max_runtime_s</strong>: sized to tank capacity (hard fault if exceeded)</li>
  <li><strong>Hardware float switch</strong> (REQUIRED): high-level switch on every destination tank — independent of ESP32 software</li>
</ol>

<h3>Responsibility Split</h3>
<div class="resp-grid">
  <div class="resp-col">
    <strong>ESP32 (On-Device)</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>State machine + valve sequencing + pump relay</li>
      <li>Flow watchdog (unconditional on every route)</li>
      <li>Per-route max runtime + API watchdog</li>
      <li>Pre-flight validation (source/dest levels)</li>
      <li>Pump relay guard (blocks direct toggle)</li>
      <li>Tank sensor suppression during pump operation</li>
      <li>HA event notifications (pump_stopped, pump_fault)</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="resp-col">
    <strong>Home Assistant (Server)</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>Operating mode selection</li>
      <li>Active threshold resolution</li>
      <li>Auto-refill trigger + conservation stop</li>
      <li>Duration timer (user-configurable)</li>
      <li>Dashboard, notifications, logging</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>HA Event Notifications</h3>
<p>The ESP32 fires <code>esphome.pump_event</code> on every state transition:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Event Type</th><th>Data Fields</th><th>When</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>stopped</code></td><td>route, reason</td><td>Clean stop (manual, tank full)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>fault</code></td><td>route, fault</td><td>Any fault condition</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

{{#if hasFlowSensors}}
<h3>Sensor Fault Detection</h3>
<p>Each flow sensor has built-in fault detection. When the pump is RUNNING and flow was previously confirmed (readings &gt; 0.5 L/min), the firmware counts consecutive zero readings:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>3 consecutive zeros</strong> (at {{timing.update_interval}} update interval) → <code>sensor_fault</code> binary sensor becomes <code>true</code> in HA</li>
  <li>A single zero can occur due to air pockets, pulse counter timing, or electrical noise — 3 consecutive readings rules out transients</li>
  <li>Counter resets to 0 on any non-zero reading or when the pump returns to IDLE</li>
</ul>
<p>This is distinct from the <strong>no_flow</strong> fault: no_flow fires when flow is <em>never established</em> after start. Sensor fault fires when flow <em>was confirmed then dropped to zero</em>, suggesting the sensor stopped reporting rather than flow actually stopping.</p>
{{/if}}

<h2>System Alerts</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Fault notification on any route entering FAULT state</li>
{{#if hasMultipleLevelTanks}}
  <li>Water critical alert when combined tank level drops below 35%</li>
{{/if}}
</ul>

<div class="installation">
<h2>Installation Guidelines</h2>

{{#if hasFlowSensors}}
<h3>Flow Sensors</h3>

<h4>Placement — The 10D/5D Rule</h4>
<p>Flow sensors require straight, undisturbed pipe upstream and downstream for accurate readings:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>10D upstream</strong>: 10 pipe diameters of straight pipe before the sensor</li>
  <li><strong>5D downstream</strong>: 5 pipe diameters of straight pipe after the sensor</li>
</ul>
<p>For a 1" (25mm) pipe: 250mm straight before, 125mm straight after.</p>
<p>Avoid placing sensors immediately after:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Elbows, tees, or reducers</li>
  <li>Partially open valves</li>
  <li>Pump outlets (turbulence)</li>
</ul>

<h4>Float Valve Requirement</h4>
<p>A float valve <strong>must</strong> be installed upstream of each flow sensor on routes filling a destination tank. When the tank is full, the float valve closes and flow drops to zero — this is how MajiFlow detects tank-full and triggers a clean stop via the flow watchdog.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Without a float valve, there is no backpressure signal and the system cannot detect a full tank</li>
  <li>The float valve is a passive mechanical device — it does not appear in the MajiFlow topology</li>
</ul>

<h4>Mounting Orientation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Mount <strong>horizontally</strong> with the impeller axis vertical</li>
  <li>Ensure the arrow on the sensor body matches flow direction</li>
  <li>The sensor must be fully flooded (no air pockets)</li>
</ul>

<h4>Sensor Sizing</h4>
<p>Match the sensor to the pipe diameter. An oversized sensor in a small pipe will under-read; an undersized sensor will create excessive pressure drop.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Sensor Model</th><th>Pipe Size</th><th>Flow Range</th><th>Pulses/L</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>YF-S201</td><td>1/2" (DN15)</td><td>1–30 L/min</td><td>~450</td></tr>
    <tr><td>YF-S401</td><td>1" (DN25)</td><td>0.3–10 L/min</td><td>~5880</td></tr>
    <tr><td>YF-DN50</td><td>2" (DN50)</td><td>10–200 L/min</td><td>~55</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Calibrate <code>flow_cal</code> (pulses/L) for your specific sensor — manufacturer values are approximate.</p>

<h4>Electrical</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Use shielded cable for runs &gt; 2m to reduce electrical noise</li>
  <li>Pull-up resistor (10kΩ to 3.3V) is recommended if not built into the sensor</li>
  <li>MajiFlow configures <code>INPUT_PULLUP</code> by default in ESPHome</li>
</ul>
{{/if}}

{{#if hasValves}}
<h3>Valves</h3>

<h4>Motorized Ball Valves</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Mount <strong>horizontally</strong> or with the actuator <strong>above</strong> the pipe (never below — condensation risk)</li>
  <li>Ensure the valve is accessible for manual override</li>
  <li>Default travel time: <strong>15 seconds</strong> — adjust <code>valve_travel_time</code> in the topology if using faster/slower actuators</li>
</ul>

<h4>Wiring</h4>
<ul>
  <li>2-wire control: one wire for OPEN direction, one for CLOSE direction</li>
  <li><strong>Never energize both directions simultaneously</strong> — MajiFlow generates ESPHome interlocks to prevent this</li>
  <li>Wire gauge: 18 AWG minimum for runs up to 15m; 16 AWG for longer runs</li>
  <li>Relay/MOSFET board must handle the motor stall current (typically 0.5–1.5A)</li>
</ul>
{{/if}}

{{#if hasTanks}}
<h3>Tank Level Sensors</h3>

<h4>Pressure-Based Level Sensing (ADC)</h4>
<p>MajiFlow uses analog pressure sensors at the tank bottom to measure water column height.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Mount at the <strong>lowest point</strong> of the tank, or on a standpipe connected to the bottom</li>
  <li>The sensor must be <strong>below</strong> the minimum water level</li>
  <li>Use a bleed valve or T-fitting for easy sensor removal/calibration</li>
</ul>

<h4>Calibration</h4>
<p>Calibrate via Home Assistant number entities:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Cal Empty (V)</strong>: Voltage reading when tank is empty</li>
  <li><strong>Cal Full (V)</strong>: Voltage reading when tank is full</li>
</ol>
<p>MajiFlow generates these as adjustable HA entities. The firmware calculates percentage as:<br>
<code>pct = clamp((voltage - v_empty) / (v_full - v_empty) × 100, 0, 100)</code></p>

<h4>Reading Suppression</h4>
<p>Tank readings are <strong>suppressed during pump operation</strong> (PREPARING, RUNNING, STOPPING states) because the pump creates pressure artifacts. Levels are only trusted when the pump is IDLE or FAULT.</p>
{{/if}}

{{#if hasPressureSensors}}
<h3>Pressure Sensors</h3>

<h4>Water Source Pressure Monitoring</h4>
<p>For WaterSource nodes with a pressure pin, an ADC pressure sensor monitors incoming supply pressure (mains, borehole).</p>
<ul>
  <li>Mount <strong>after</strong> any pressure regulator and <strong>before</strong> the first branch/valve</li>
  <li>Use a sensor rated for the expected pressure range (typically 0–10 bar for mains)</li>
  <li>Install a snubber or damper if the supply has water hammer</li>
</ul>

<h4>Sensor Selection</h4>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Range</th><th>Output</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>0–10 bar, 0.5–4.5V</td><td>Mains supply</td><td>Analog (ADC)</td><td>Most common for residential</td></tr>
    <tr><td>0–6 bar, 4–20mA</td><td>Low pressure</td><td>Requires signal converter</td><td>Better noise immunity</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h4>Maintenance</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Isolation valve</strong>: Install a manual isolation valve upstream of each pressure sensor to allow replacement or calibration without draining the system.</li>
  <li>This is a manual (non-actuated) valve — it does not appear in the MajiFlow topology.</li>
</ul>
{{/if}}

<h3>General Piping Notes</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pipe sizing</strong>: Match pipe diameter to expected flow rates. Undersized pipes increase pressure drop and reduce sensor accuracy.</li>
  <li><strong>Pressure regulators</strong>: Install upstream of the system if mains pressure exceeds component ratings (typically &gt;6 bar).</li>
  <li><strong>Check valves</strong>: Consider non-return valves to prevent backflow, especially on tank inlet lines.</li>
  <li><strong>Strainers/filters</strong>: Install upstream of flow sensors to prevent debris from fouling the impeller.</li>
  <li><strong>Float switches</strong>: Required on every destination tank as a hardware safety interlock independent of the ESP32.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Electrical Notes</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Relay board</strong>: Must handle the total inrush current of all simultaneously energized actuators</li>
  <li><strong>Power supply</strong>: 12V or 24V DC for valve motors; 3.3V logic for ESP32 GPIOs</li>
  <li><strong>Grounding</strong>: Ensure all metallic pipes and the relay board share a common ground</li>
  <li><strong>Wire runs</strong>: Keep signal wires (sensors) separated from power wires (motors, pump relay) to reduce interference</li>
</ul>
</div>

